 loc='\U0001f4cd Borger, on the Hondsrug between Gieten and Exloo \u00b7 Esdorp with surrounding landscape \u00b7 Large area',
 desc='<b>Borger</b> is called the <b>dolmen capital</b> of the Netherlands for good reason. On the edge of the village lies <b>D27</b>, the largest dolmen in the country: nearly 23 metres long, built of 47 stones the heaviest of which weighs tens of tonnes, and raised around <b>3350 BC</b> by the Funnel Beaker culture. Beside it stands the <b>Hunebedcentrum</b>, the museum and information point of the UNESCO Geopark. But Borger is more than its dolmen: it is an intact <b>esdorp</b> with an elongated green, old farmhouses and a domed arable field still in use. Around the village lie the landscapes of the ridge \u2014 <b>woods, heath remnants, pools</b> \u2014 and on the east side the land falls away to the <b>Hunze valley</b>, where the great brook-valley restoration is under way. In the woods near Borger there are also several lesser-known dolmens and burial mounds, often unsignposted, that you simply come across among the pines on a walk.',
